course: dictionary
lesson: 10336
title: Tram: a word from the city
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A tram is a public vehicle that carries passengers along city streets. It runs on rails, which are metal tracks set into the road, and it gets its power from electricity carried by a wire above it. «A tram takes electricity from an overhead wire through a pole on its roof.»
explain: Context helps you work out what tram means. «Take the tram» and «by tram» show it is a way to travel, and «tram stop» shows that people wait there. The clue words are rails, passengers and stop, so you can guess the meaning before you check it.
explain: The sentences also tell you how a tram behaves. «A tram runs on rails and cannot swerve.» Because it cannot steer around things, you must look carefully before you cross the tram lines. «Bike wheels can get stuck in tram rails, so cyclists cross them at an angle.»
explain: Some words for the same thing change from place to place. In North America a tram is often called a streetcar or a trolley, and cities such as Melbourne, Manchester and Dublin have trams too. When you meet a new word, read the clues around it, then check in a dictionary.
fact: A tram is a public passenger vehicle that runs on rails along city streets.
fact: Trams get their power from electricity carried by an overhead wire.
fact: In North America, a tram can also be called a streetcar or a trolley.
fact: «Tram lines» are the rails a tram runs on, and cyclists should cross them at an angle.
fact: A tram cannot swerve, so people walking, cycling and driving must look out for it.
exercise: «A tram runs on rails in the street.» What is a tram? | A bus with no engine | * A public vehicle that runs on rails along city streets | A small car with four wheels | A boat that sails on the river
exercise: «A tram takes electricity from an overhead wire through a pole on its roof.» What does overhead mean? | * Above you, up high | Very fast | In the back of the tram | Under the ground
exercise: In North America, what is another name for a tram? | Pavement | * A streetcar | A bicycle | A lorry
test: «A tram cannot swerve out of the way.» What does swerve mean? | Go backwards slowly | Stop completely and wait | * Move suddenly to one side to avoid something | Ring a bell to warn people
test: Ella says a tram can steer round a parked car just like a car can. What is the right answer? | No, trams cannot move at all | Yes, trams steer just like cars | Yes, because trams are very quiet | * No, a tram runs on rails and cannot swerve, so people must look out for it
exam: Which word tells you a tram gets its power from above? | Rails | * Overhead | Passenger | Street
